"Clive Crous" <clive@linuxgames.co.za> wrote:

> that's my nethack-imagination working overtime ;)

For my vision, I hearken back to Adventure, Zork,
and Hunt the Wumpus, where it was more obvious that
the dungeons were "graphs", consisting of
connections and locations, each location having a
list of attributes.

Just because, in Rogue and its descendents, we
_depict_ the dungeon as if it consisted of cells
that have extent doesn't make that true. It's still
just a graph, with connections between locations,
where the vast majority of those connections are N,
S, E, W, NE, NW, SE, SW ones, and the locations are
still just extentless and characterized by a list,
the list containing both entities ("five daggars")
and attributes ("wall", "rock", "undiscovered secret
door", "floor").

The lists, and their entities and attributes, have
rules, one of which is that live things don't stack,
one of which is that boulders can only be "dropped"
into a space containing a boulder, not "pushed", and
so on.

That doesn't map very nicely onto the "real world",
but NetHack is a game, it isn't required to model
the real world beyond what minimal amount is needed
for game play.

